Materials and methods
Data source
Data were sourced from Nphos 2.0 (unpublished). Nphos 2.0 currently contains 28 629 N-phosphorylation sites from 16 614 proteins across 419 species. These include 18 990 N-phosphorylation sites from 11 341 proteins in eukaryotic species, and 9639 N-phosphorylation sites from 5273 proteins in prokaryotic species. O-phosphorylation protein and site data were collected from qPhos [
71
], qPTM [
72
], qPTMplants [
73
], dbPTM [
74
], iPTMnet [
75
], and PhosphoSitePlus [
76
] databases (March 2024). All sites and datasets have been reprocessed and curated. The specific steps for data collection and procession are as follows: we searched PubMed, bioRxiv, and ProteomeXchange [
77
] (
http://www.proteomexchange.org/
, repository for MS data) using multiple keywords such as “pHis,” “pArg,” “pLys,” “protein histidine phosphorylation,” “protein arginine phosphorylation,” “protein lysine phosphorylation,” “phosphoarginine,” “phosphohistidine,” “phospholysine,”
et al.
In total, 553 articles were eventually retrieved as of March 2024. Raw MS data were obtained from the ProteomeXchange consortium and re-analyzed using MaxQuant [
78
] (v2.5.0.0) with search parameters matching the original experimental conditions (e.g. protease). The species-specific UniProt [
79
] proteomes (March 2024 release) were used as the background sequence dataset for spectral matching. Peptide-spectrum matches were validated at Percolator [
80
] FDR threshold of 1%. In parallel, we extracted literature-supported protein N-phosphorylation site information from UniProt (March 2024 release). Given the continuous updates of protein sequences in both UniProt and NCBI databases, we re-calibrated and consolidated the collected protein N-phosphorylation sites from the literature with the downloaded O-phosphorylation site.
Functional analysis
Species with >10 detected N-phosphorylated proteins (
Supplementary Table S1
) were selected for functional enrichment analysis. GO and subcellular localization data for these species were obtained from UniProt (March 2025). Protein classification data were acquired from the PANTHER database (v19,
http://pantherdb.org/
) [
81
]. Metabolic pathway information, disease associations, and EIP data were retrieved from KEGG (
https://www.genome.jp/kegg/
, March 2025) [
82
]. Enrichment analysis of N-phosphorylated proteins in corresponding species was performed using the clusterProfiler [
83
] (v4.10.0, R 4.3.2) with parameters:
P
value Cutoff ≤.1,
P
AdjustMethod = “BH” (Benjamini–Hochberg correction).
Usage calculation
Based on the collected N-phosphorylation and O-phosphorylation data, the number of six amino acid residues (Ser, Thr, Tyr, Arg, Lys, and His) and six phosphorylated amino acid residues (pSer, pThr, pTyr, pArg, pLys, and pHis) per phosphorylated protein was counted. The amino acid usage (Usage
i
) and phosphorylated amino acid occurrence rate (Occurrence rate
pi
) were calculated as follows:
